Z||000003||Monkeyman||09-19-2001||07:43 PM||||Is that a Grant?  Looks very similar to mine.  I like the fact that it sticks out farther from the dash.  (And the fact that it isn't quite as large as a pizza.  Darn stock school bus steering wheel.)||||reg||1
Z||000004||Kasdan||09-19-2001||07:48 PM||||I have that same setup, same exact wheel actually....<P>------------------<BR>Tyler Morgan, 1986 GT<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/Fiero21.jpg">         <BR>Power Steering, Holley Scoop<BR>Aim=FieroDriver1986||||reg||1
Z||000005||California Kid||09-19-2001||08:10 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by sqoach:<BR><B>Only thing I don't like about it is that it sticks out a little further than the stock wheel, due to the adapter you have to get. There has to be some way to fix that. But I can live with it for now.   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><BR>Had the same problem and modified the original equipment steel closure "cone" (painted black) with a backing plate, drilled and tapped for the six to eight screws of adapter "collar" (painted wrinkle black). Hence, adapter "collar" is bolted to "cone". The "cone" is sandwiched between steering shaft adapter and steering wheel using the common 3 fastener points.<P>Hope this helps you out, or gives you ideas to modify your own. It took me a little while to figure out how to modify mine and keep the wheel from flexing, but it's real solid. The trick is to modify your original closure cone or make a new thicker one with tapped mount holes.<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/Steering_Wheel_a.jpg"> <BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/Steering_Wheel.jpg"> <BR>Results in position very close to original and feels great with smaller diameter wheel.<P><P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/Signature_5.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000006||sqoach||09-19-2001||08:16 PM||||Final price including shipping it here, and wheel adaptor was around $70. And yes it is a Grant wheel. The reason I don't like it sticking out further is because I'm not real tall, so my seat isn't back very far. If I were taller and need the seat back pretty far, it'd be perfect. The adapter makes it stick out about two inches more than a stock wheel. But I like it anyway. And the smaller diameter is nice.||||reg||1
Z||000007||sqoach||09-19-2001||08:18 PM||||Looks like I started typing as you posted California Kid. That's exactly what I'm wanting to do. I'll take a look at it tomorrow to see what I can come up with. Thanks.||||reg||1
Z||000008||California Kid||09-19-2001||09:18 PM||||  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"> You're Welcome.  Couldn't stand mine with the adapter, have long arms, and it felt like the wheel was into my chest.<P>By the way mine's a Grant also, used same adapter kit you have (recognized the plastic closure tube).<p>[This message has been edited by California Kid (edited 09-19-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000009||AusFiero||09-20-2001||09:14 AM||||I also use adaptors rather than complete boss kits. Mine is in the exact original position. Reason. The boss kits for Fieros aren't available in Aus.<P>------------------<BR>    <IMG SRC="http://www.jadeweb.com.au/fiero/ausfiero1.gif">   <BR><A HREF="http://www.jadeweb.com.au/fiero" TARGET=_blank>My Fiero</A> | <A HREF="http://www.fieroshop.com.au" TARGET=_blank>Fiero Accessories</A>||202.139.20.3||reg||1
Z||000010||pherder||09-20-2001||09:59 AM||||Grant makes a 1" spacer that can be used instead of the 3" piece that comes with the wheel and bolts to the adapter<P>I used that on my 308 kit and the wheel ended up being in the stock position.<P>Jegs catalog lists it as:  <BR>470-4001   1" Extension Kit $15.99<P>Paul<P>------------------<BR>--<BR>Paul Herder<BR>88 Formula - Artero kit car doner and 350 TPI<BR>86 SE - 3.2 w/440T4 Transaxle<BR>95 GMC - 350 TBI - Factory "Custom"<BR>92 Mazda Protege - Stock<BR>55 Jones - First Edition WIFE- all mods, equipment,updates :D<BR>Sedgwick, KS 67135||||reg||1
Z||000011||Alex4mula||09-20-2001||10:44 AM||||Paul posted exactly what I was going to say. I also didn't like the 3" adapter. Lived with that for over 5 years until I knew about the adapter. It puts it in the stock location. For $15 is worth every penny for me.||||reg||1
